8:     8	A thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare evidence within its thesis to repeat, replace or add. Extra source references alone do not trigger rejudgement. Extraction determines group associations; the writer preserves them after resolving final thesis IDs under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction).
12:    12	- Clear structures connect theses, ladders and evidence to source items.
13:    13	- A thesis is a question with a state, not an assertion. Opposing views move the same state's direction rather than creating opposite duplicate theses.
14:    14	- A fixed, verifiable ladder states in advance what would disprove the view, analogous to explicit analyst scenarios or predefined investment exit conditions. Do not change it after observing the outcome.
16:    16	- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.
17:    17	- A thesis contains one period and one due date, generally 6–12 months; intermediate quarterly reports are evidence. A thesis on a binding obligation a universe company takes on (a guarantee, financing, an equity stake, a purchase commitment) may set its own check day up to 18 months after the item, because the signing can be checked long before what it finances is built (D185).
18:    18	- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.
30:    30	Thesis library
32:    32	│   └─ Thesis → A question + a ladder + a due date (Immutable once written. Five rungs, or two for yes/no. When matching opens a thesis, only one may be open for the same subject and focus at a time.)
33:    33	│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model judges from facts. A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)
35:    35	│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)
36:    36	│       └─ Succession → The previous period's thesis (Successive periods of the same focus form a chain)
38:    38	└─ Append-only records → Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labelling records (Append at every step, without editing or deleting. Read records up to a historical date to recover its judgement.)
42:    42	## Two thesis kinds
45:    45	| Kind | Subject kind | Ladder rungs | Example |
47:    47	| Asset thesis | Tradable subjects: companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities | Five rungs: strongly bullish, bullish, neutral, bearish, strongly bearish. Two for yes/no: bullish and bearish. These describe the business impact; set them at creation and keep them fixed. | How will NVDA FY2027 China revenue compare with FY2026? |
48:    48	| Theme thesis | Macro, policy and industry themes | Ordered outcomes from one end to the other: five rungs, or two for yes/no. Name outcomes without judging them good or bad. Dependent asset theses specify which outcome benefits them. | How will the Fed's target-rate upper bound after its last 2026 meeting compare with now? (Large cut … large hike) |
53:    53	## Ladder
55:    55	<!-- generated: ladder -->
57:    57	Two kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)
58:    58	├─ Asset thesis: numeric → NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 | Five rungs describing NVDA's business impact (Due on the FY2027 annual earnings date)
64:    64	└─ Theme thesis: event → China export licensing for chips covered by the April 2025 rule, including H20 and MI308, as of April 15, 2026 | Five ordered outcomes, without good/bad labels (Use the rules in force on that date)
72:    72	1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed.
74:    74	3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.
75:    75	4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.
76:    76	5. Numeric theses name a year. Non-company quantities use calendar years: the publication year is this year and the following year is next year, each ending December 31. Company quantities use the fiscal year containing publication day and its successor. Never use the computer clock. For a given quantity, open at most one thesis for each of this year and next year; do not open later years. The due date is the quantity's publication date.
78:    78	<!-- /generated: ladder -->
85:    85	| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges at low temperature from facts. Reconsider all evidence still in force, including each item's replaced predecessor to show the change, plus current dependency states and reasons and, for an asset thesis, the theme block. Do not increment or decrement the previous score. Cite evidence and compare with the conclusion before this round to state whether it changed. Do not rejudge if only source references were added and dependency conclusions did not change. | After the 8-K: strongly bearish |
